# alb hub environment.
# Copy this file to hub.env (same folder), fill in ALB_AGENT_TOKEN,
# then start the hub with ./run-hub.sh
# On a multi-user host keep the token to yourself:  chmod 600 hub.env
#
# This file is sourced by the shell, so quote values that contain spaces
# or '$'. Tokens made of letters/digits/_/- never need quoting — and paste
# cleanly into the agent's agent.conf too.

# Shared agent token — must match `token` in the agent's agent.conf.
# Generate one:  openssl rand -hex 16
ALB_AGENT_TOKEN=change-me

# Serial console: the COM port the agent should open, and the baud rate.
# Chosen here on the hub (it drives the agent). Leave commented for adb-only.
#ALB_AGENT_SERIAL_COM=COM27
#ALB_AGENT_SERIAL_BAUD=1500000

# Several boards on the bench (ADR-063): list each board's COM port and baud
# in a TOML file (see boards.toml.example) and switch with
# `alb bench board use <name>` while the hub runs. A chosen board overrides
# the two lines above; with none chosen they apply as before.
#ALB_BENCH_BOARDS=deploy/hub/boards.toml

# Where the API listens. The Windows agent must be able to reach this host,
# so the default binds all interfaces (127.0.0.1 would lock the agent out).
# Note: only /agent/* checks the token — the rest of the API has no auth,
# so keep the hub on a trusted network.
#ALB_API_HOST=0.0.0.0
#ALB_API_PORT=8765

# The adb forwarder binds 127.0.0.1:5037 — the same port a local adb server
# uses. If this hub runs its own adb server, either `adb kill-server` here
# or move the forwarder to a free port.
#
# Since ADR-057 you do NOT then export ADB_SERVER_SOCKET to match: alb reads
# this port from the running hub, so it stays correct wherever you move the
# forwarder. A hand-copied value is a second source of truth, and when it goes
# stale the symptom is "0 devices" — indistinguishable from an unplugged board.
# That mistake cost a full day on 2026-08-10. Leave ADB_SERVER_SOCKET unset.
#ALB_ADB_FORWARD_PORT=15037

# Serial forwarder port override (default 9001). If you move it, also point
# alb at the new port with the second line — this file covers the web UI
# (alb-api), but a separately launched alb / alb-mcp needs ALB_SERIAL_TCP in
# its own environment too.
#ALB_SERIAL_FORWARD_PORT=19001
#ALB_SERIAL_TCP=127.0.0.1:19001

# WebSocket compression (permessage-deflate) is OFF by default (ADR-062).
# The hub compresses every frame in its own event loop, which made it — not
# the network — the limit: a 2.2 GB image took 108 s compressed, 19 s plain
# over a LAN. Set to 1 only when the hub-to-agent link is slow (a VPN or the
# internet) and the images compress well.
#ALB_WS_DEFLATE=1

# Start the bench agent from this hub (`alb bench agent-start`, ADR-061):
# a Host from this user's ~/.ssh/config that logs in to the bench host with
# a key, after setup-remote-start.bat has run once in the agent folder there.
# Unset = the command says it is not configured. The task name only needs
# setting if the bench host registered it under another name.
#ALB_BENCH_SSH_HOST=bench-host
#ALB_BENCH_AGENT_TASK=alb-agent
